SFArmPlatformExpert: SFAppleAIC's handler has the barriers the disable protocol needs

Audit finding H1 (docs/roadmap/research/smp-lock-audit.md). IOInterruptController's
disableInterrupt()/unregisterInterrupt() set interruptDisabledSoft, issue a barrier and
spin until interruptActive is clear; xnu's own handleInterrupt() issues a barrier after
setting interruptActive. This handler did not, so with the interrupt on CPU 0 and a
teardown on another CPU (Wi-Fi off/reload removes its event source) each side could miss
the other's store: the handler then calls into an event source that was just freed. The
handler also re-checks interruptDisabledSoft after marking the source hard disabled, so
that an enableInterrupt() racing with the end of the handler cannot leave the source
masked for good.

--- a/src/Kernel/Extensions/SFArmPlatformExpert/SFAppleAIC.cpp
+++ b/src/Kernel/Extensions/SFArmPlatformExpert/SFAppleAIC.cpp
@@ -258,6 +258,15 @@
 
 		IOInterruptVector *vector = &vectors[irq];
 		vector->interruptActive = 1;
+		/*
+		 * As IOInterruptController::handleInterrupt has it: disableInterrupt()
+		 * and unregisterInterrupt() on another CPU set interruptDisabledSoft,
+		 * issue a barrier and then wait for interruptActive to clear. Without
+		 * a barrier between our store and the load below both sides can miss
+		 * the other's store, and the handler runs on a vector that is being
+		 * torn down (its event source freed).
+		 */
+		OSMemoryBarrier();
 
 		if (!vector->interruptDisabledSoft && vector->interruptRegistered) {
 			vector->handler(vector->target, vector->refCon,
@@ -269,6 +278,18 @@
 			 */
 			if (vector->interruptDisabledSoft) {
 				vector->interruptDisabledHard = 1;
+				/*
+				 * enableInterrupt() on another CPU clears DisabledSoft
+				 * and then looks at DisabledHard to decide whether to
+				 * unmask. If it cleared Soft after our test and read
+				 * Hard before our store, nobody would unmask the
+				 * source again: look once more and unmask it here.
+				 */
+				OSMemoryBarrier();
+				if (!vector->interruptDisabledSoft) {
+					vector->interruptDisabledHard = 0;
+					enableVector(irq, vector);
+				}
 			} else {
 				enableVector(irq, vector);
 			}
@@ -277,6 +298,7 @@
 			disableVectorHard(irq, vector);
 		}
 
+		OSMemoryBarrier();
 		vector->interruptActive = 0;
 	}
 
